  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 1053 AM CST SAT NOV 14 2026\n\nOn the synoptic scale, a split-flow pattern is evident at 500 mb\nacross the central and eastern CONUS. A large confluence zone\nwill support a large area of surface high pressure across the \neastern half of the country for the next several days. For \ntonight, the weakening surface pressure gradient associated with a\nsurface ridge axis, will promote strong radiational cooling. A \nwidespread frost and freeze is expected with morning lows on \nSunday ranging from the upper 20s to lower 30s across the entire \nforecast area. High clouds will increase on Sunday ahead of an \napproaching shortwave within the southern jet stream, but \nmeasurable precipitation should remain south of our forecast area \nSunday night into Monday morning, due to the suppressed nature of\nthis system and the presence of low-level dry air.\n\nThe current airmass will persist for several days as surface high\npressure becomes entrenched in the central and southern\nAppalachians, and temperatures will gradually moderate through\nTuesday, most notably in the afternoon hours. Eventually we can\nexpect temperatures to rebound into the lower 70s for Wednesday\nthrough Friday as an upper-level ridge builds across the region.\n\n87/Grantham",
